# The guest kernel for a sandbox that runs untrusted code.
#
# .github/workflows/guest-kernel.yml builds it, and this is the only
# fragment order that produces it:
#
#   KERNEL_EXTRA_CONFIG="container.config vsock.config smp.config \
#       rng.config virtiofs.config sandbox.config" \
#       KERNEL_OUT_SUFFIX=-sandbox ./build.sh 6.12.13
#
# Why each fragment is in the list:
#
#   container.config  The payload is a container. It needs one namespace
#                     set per container, cgroup v2 accounting, netfilter
#                     and veth.
#   vsock.config      AF_VSOCK and its virtio transport. This is the
#                     control channel between the host and the in-guest
#                     agent. virtio-console does not multiplex, so it
#                     cannot carry exec, logs and stats at once.
#   smp.config        More than one vCPU, and CONFIG_CPUSETS, which
#                     depends on SMP. CPUSETS also sits below the CGROUPS
#                     menu, so it needs container.config as well, and
#                     olddefconfig drops it without a message when
#                     either fragment is missing.
#   rng.config        virtio-rng. Untrusted code does TLS, and
#                     fastboot.config leaves hwrng out, so the device
#                     enumerates and nothing binds to it.
#   virtiofs.config   FUSE, virtio-fs and overlayfs. The root filesystem
#                     is a read-only host export with a tmpfs upper
#                     layer, so the guest writes without touching it.
#   sandbox.config    This file. It must be last, because kconfig keeps
#                     the last assignment and this fragment turns an
#                     option off that an earlier fragment turns on by
#                     default.
#
# hotplug.config is deliberately not in the list. It sets CONFIG_SMP and
# CONFIG_NR_CPUS itself (see hotplug.config:7-9), so with smp.config the
# vCPU count would depend on fragment order.

# ── Containment: no vsock path inside the guest ─────────────────
# CONFIG_VSOCKETS_LOOPBACK is "default y" under CONFIG_VSOCKETS, so
# olddefconfig turns it on as soon as vsock.config lands. It gives any
# guest process a loopback transport to CID 1 and to the guest's own
# CID, which reaches the agent's vsock port from inside the sandbox. The
# agent answers the host, and only the host. Without this transport the
# vsock core sends such a connection to the guest-to-host transport,
# where the VMM refuses it.
# CONFIG_VSOCKETS_LOOPBACK is not set
